WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.118
Five Treasury subdomains

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:02.849
taken over for

00:00:02.661 --> 00:00:04.005
three dollars each

00:00:04.542 --> 00:00:05.862
Researchers cycled

00:00:05.674 --> 00:00:07.419
through AWS Elastic

00:00:07.231 --> 00:00:08.008
IPs

00:00:08.056 --> 00:00:08.987
and took over

00:00:08.799 --> 00:00:10.131
five US Treasury

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:10.850
subdomains

00:00:11.051 --> 00:00:12.171
then reported and

00:00:11.983 --> 00:00:12.914
fixed them through

00:00:12.726 --> 00:00:13.716
a bug bounty

00:00:14.254 --> 00:00:15.621
A dangling DNS

00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:16.730
record pointing at

00:00:16.542 --> 00:00:17.673
a released Elastic

00:00:17.485 --> 00:00:18.098
IP

00:00:18.204 --> 00:00:19.383
lets anyone claim

00:00:19.195 --> 00:00:19.926
that address

00:00:19.985 --> 00:00:20.645
and with it

00:00:20.457 --> 00:00:21.494
the subdomain

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:22.977
They covered under

00:00:22.788 --> 00:00:23.684
one percent of

00:00:23.496 --> 00:00:24.522
the address space

00:00:24.451 --> 00:00:25.477
and still found

00:00:25.288 --> 00:00:27.175
28 high-impact takeovers

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:28.000
so check your

00:00:27.812 --> 00:00:29.168
DNS records now

00:00:29.694 --> 00:00:30.708
Full story on

00:00:30.519 --> 00:00:31.415
the dashboard
